{
  "id": "p9kyl7b",
  "title": "CHAPTER 3: DATABASE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS (DBMS)",
  "order": 3,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "x25hxh0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.1 Introduction and Key Concepts"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The ANSI/SPARC 3-tier architecture separates physical storage from user views using Internal, Conceptual, and External schemas.",
        "Entity-Relationship (ER) modeling utilizes entities, attributes, and cardinality constraints to conceptualize database designs.",
        "Relational models enforce data integrity through Primary, Foreign, Candidate, and Super Keys.",
        "SQL commands are categorized into DDL (structure), DML (data manipulation), DQL (querying), DCL (permissions), and TCL (transactions).",
        "Normalisation eliminates redundant data and prevents Insertion, Update, and Deletion anomalies across relational tables.",
        "Database transactions depend on ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) and concurrency controls for reliability."
      ],
      "id": "1zu5ys0"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
